  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1056 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nAs of Saturday evening, we are entering the coldest 2 day period\nof the forecast, as the influx of continental polar air continues\nunder northwest flow aloft. The pattern will begin to deamplify\nby Sunday as a longwave upper level trough over the eastern CONUS\nbegins to lift offshore.  During this period, a surface pressure\nridge will settle into the TN River Valley Saturday night,\nsetting up another favorable radiational cooling night. Latest\nNBM deterministic lows are around the 25th percentile of the\nguidance envelope for this weekend's lows temps, which appears\nreasonable.\n\nBy Sunday afternoon, weak ridging aloft will translate east from\nthe Great Plains to Middle MS River Valley. Global model\nconsensus depicts a weakening cut-off low moving from the Red\nRiver Valley to the central Gulf coast Sunday night into early\nMonday. This feature's impacts for the Midsouth will be limited\nto increased midlevel cloud cover on Sunday.\n\nAn upstream upper ridge will amplify from TX into the upper\nMidwest on Tuesday, in advance of a progressive longwave trough\nmoving into the Great Basin. 500mb heights will peak around\n585dam over the Midsouth on Wednesday, roughly 16dam above normal\nfor mid/late November. By this time, daytime highs will warm into\nthe mid/upper 60s. Thursday morning appears to be our first\nopportunity for above freezing low temperatures across the\nMidsouth, including areas near the Tennessee River.\n\nThe western U.S. trough will lift into the central and northern\nplains by next weekend, effectively flattening but not displacing\nthe upper level ridge over the Lower MS River Valley and the\nSoutheast. As such, the mild temps will continue across the\nMidsouth, as rain chances remain limited.",
